            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>

I had identified many locations of excess air leakage (heat loss) from your home:



** The older windows and doors



** A few sections of the header space of the basement, where the pink foam insulation has been compromised



** The attic hatch can be sealed with a "removable" caulking, such as Draft Attack



** The chimney for the existing furnace. Please note that the flue vent damper in the chimney is missing and this can result in 2 significant problems:

       1. Combustion fumes from the furnace spilling into the house; and

       2. Excess heat loss from a 6" diameter pipe allowing continuous air leakage up &amp; out of the house



Savings: I have modeled a modest 20% reduction in overall air leakage, that could potentially save $ 760 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 20.8 GJ, and qualify for $ 750 in rebates. For homeowners participating in the DHERP, a minimum 10% reduction in air leakage would qualify for a $ 500 rebate. Achieving 20% qualifies for $750, and a minimum 30% reduction qualifies for $1,000.



To qualify for a Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.76 (R 10.0). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. To be eligible for a rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, you must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program

</airSeal>
                <mainWalls>

The exterior walls of the house are 2x4@16" with R10 insulation. If you are considering an exterior siding &amp; windows upgrade, this is a great time to add more exterior wall insulation.



Under the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP) that was launched on April 1, 2019, there are 3 tiers of rebates associated with how much insulation is added ($3,000 for adding R10 - R14, $5,000 for adding R15 - R19, and $8,000 for adding R20+). PLEASE NOTE: These rebates are for fully detached housing - for duplex houses (where there is one common wall between the units), there are only 3 exterior walls that will have the additional insulation installed. For this reason, the wall insulation rebate is adjusted to 75%.



Most homeowners participating in this program are already committed to (or seriously considering) an exterior siding + new windows retrofit ............ so our rebate is meant to help cover some of the cost of the additional insulation to go behind that new siding.



Our DHERP will be expiring on March 31, 2024, so there is really only this summer &amp; next summer for you to complete this project .......... if you want to qualify through this program.



For your house, I modeled the adding another R15 to 100% of the exterior walls of the house.



Savings:

By increasing 100% of the exterior walls by R15, you could potentially save $ 642 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 17.6 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 3,750 (75% of $5,000)  in rebates from the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP). Adding R20 would increase the rebate to $6,000 (75% of $8,000).



To qualify for a Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.76 (R 10.0). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. To be eligible for a rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, you must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program</mainWalls>
                <ceilings>

The current attic insulation is about R20. I have modeled an increase up to a minimum R50.



By increasing the attic insulation from R20 up to a minimum R50, you could potentially save $ 195 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 5.4 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 750 from the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP). If you do not complete a DHERP eligible wall insulation upgrade, you could still be eligible for up to $ 375 from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



NOTE: Please see the "Additional Comments" section of this report for important information about the DHERP, and your eligibility to participate.



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors.



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



NOTE: The GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program is regularly reviewed and the rebate amount and/or eligibility criteria can change. In addition, rebate funding is provided on a first-come, first-serve basis contingent upon available funding. Please check our website before purchasing to determine current rebate amounts, eligibility criteria and if rebate funding is still available.

</ceilings>
                <foundation>

See my comments &amp; estimated savings in the previous Wall Insulation upgrade section of this report.</foundation>
                <windows>

All 10 of the windows in your house are older wood windows, including the 3 in the basement that have been boarded over. Many of the windows do not seal well and allow alot of air leakage heat loss. I recommend you install ENERGY STAR?????? qualified, full frame replacement windows that are triple pane, double low-E, argon gas filled, hinged or fixed-frame windows, with a U-Value (rate of heat loss) no higher than 0.21 BTU per hour.



Savings:

By replacing all 10 windows with ones that meet the criteria listed above, you could potentially save $ 437 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 12.0 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 2,500 in rebates (10 windows max @ $250 each) through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP) if you complete an eligible wall insulation upgrade.



To qualify for a Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.76 (R10.0). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. To be eligible for a rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, you must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 



http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program</windows>
                <HotWaterSystem>

You currently have a 184 litre electric hot water tank. As electricity is quite expensive in Fort Providence and you essentially have a very large "electric kettle", there is an option for you to consider replacing this hot water heater with an ENERGY STAR?????? qualified instantaneous condensing gas-fired water heater with an Energy Factor of 0.95 or higher.



Savings: By replacing your current hot water tank with an ENERGY STAR?????? qualified instantaneous condensing gas-fired water heater with a thermal efficiency of a minimum 0.95 EF, could potentially save $ 1050 per year in energy costs, and qualify for up to $ 750 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.

 

For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



NOTE: The GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program is regularly reviewed and the rebate amount and/or eligibility criteria can change. In addition, rebate funding is provided on a first-come, first-serve basis contingent upon available funding. Please check our website before purchasing to determine current rebate amounts, eligibility criteria and if rebate funding is still available.

</HotWaterSystem>
                <HeatingSystem>

Your house is currently heated with an 85.3% efficient, oil furnace. Please note that the flue vent damper in the chimney is missing and this can result in 2 significant problems:



1. Combustion fumes from the furnace spilling into the house; and

2. Excess heat loss from a 6" diameter pipe allowing continuous air leakage up &amp; out of the house.



The current furnace is pretty efficient (for oil) and not too old, so I am only modeled the option for you to upgrade to a high-efficiency condensing propane furnace in case you convert both halves of this duplex to propane. If you do, I recommend an ENERGY STAR?????? qualified propane furnace with a minimum efficiency of 96% AFUE, including a high efficiency ECM motor to circulate the heat.



Savings: By replacing your current furnace with a 96% AFUE high-efficiency condensing gas furnace, that incorporates an ECM fan blower, you could potentially save $ 938 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 23.5 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 3,000 from the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP). If you do not complete a DHERP eligible wall insulation upgrade, you could still be eligible for up to $ 1,500 from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



NOTE: Please see the "Additional Comments" section of this report for important information about the DHERP, and your eligibility to participate.



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors.



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



NOTE: The GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program is regularly reviewed and the rebate amount and/or eligibility criteria can change. In addition, rebate funding is provided on a first-come, first-serve basis contingent upon available funding. Please check our website before purchasing to determine current rebate amounts, eligibility criteria and if rebate funding is still available.

</HeatingSystem>
                <WaterConservation>

Your house has two toilets and the one in the basement is an older unit that can be replaced with a lower water consumption model. When replacing toilets, purchase a 4.8 Liter or less (or dual-flush) model. Look for a MAP flush rating capacity of a minimum 1000 grams per flush. Go online and check out some "Youtube" flush capacity videos.



Savings:

By replacing an older toilet with a new 4.8 Liter or less (or dual-flush) model, you could potentially save ~$50 per person, per year in water and energy savings.  There are currently no rebates available for toilets from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.</WaterConservation>
                <AdditionalComments>

Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program



To qualify for a Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP) rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total above foundation, exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.76 (R 10.0). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits, and excludes any new additions. To be eligible for a rebate through the DHERP, you must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the Arctic Energy Alliance.



DHERP rebates are provided on a first-come, first-serve basis for as long as funding remains available. Receiving a pre-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation does not in itself guarantee the availability of funding. Homeowners who have demonstrated their intention to proceed with an eligible above foundation, exterior wall insulation upgrade will be:

      A. Provided with a conditional "Pre-Authorization" of DHERP funding, if funding is still available; or

      B. Placed on a DHERP funding waiting list, should funding become available



For more information and program guidelines, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program



As you research if you will indeed participate in the DHERP, please note the upgrade recommendations outlined within this report are estimated to qualify for up to $12,750 in rebates. The following are the estimated rebates per component of the DHERP:



     - $3,750 for the wall insulation upgrade

     - $2,500 for upgrading 10 windows with eligible ESTAR units

     - $   750 for the attic insulation upgrade

     - $3,000 for an eligible furnace upgrade

     - $   750 for a 20% - 30% air leakage reduction ($1,000 if you achieve &gt;30%)

     - $2,000 Bonus if you achieve a 30% - 40% total energy reduction 



For more information, please visit our website:

https://aea.nt.ca/program/home-improvements/

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



NOTE: These programs are regularly reviewed and the rebate amounts and/or eligibility criteria can change. In addition, rebate funding is provided on a first-come, first-serve basis contingent upon available funding. Please check our website before purchasing to determine current rebate amounts, eligibility criteria and if rebate funding is still available.





-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



LED Lights



Savings: Light Emitting Diode (LED) lights save about 80% compared to regular incandescent lighting, and about 20% compared to flourescent lighting. Floursecent lighting also contains mercury which is a hazardous waste material that requires special disposal. And ..... LED lights work well in the cold and can last a long time.



Rebates: In Fort Providence, LED light bulbs and tubes are eligible for a rebate of 50% of the purchase price up to $ 400 (per fiscal year)  from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



NOTE: The GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program is regularly reviewed and the rebate amount and/or eligibility criteria can change. In addition, rebate funding is provided on a first-come, first-serve basis contingent upon available funding. Please check our website before purchasing to determine current rebate amounts, eligibility criteria and if rebate funding is still available.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Depressurization Test



The result of the exhaust devices depressurization test, which consists of turning on all exhaust equipment that vents to the outside (for your house, just the clothes dryer), indicated a 0.3 Pascal pressure drop. As this number is less than 5 Pascals, the use of this equipment is not expected to draw combustion products into your home, if you have any chimney vented appliances in your home that are not direct-vent. Your current furnace is chimney vented (and is missing the required flue vent damper) and is highly susceptible to back-drafting if the house were too air tight (not a current concern of this).



During my assessment of your home, I noticed one very old smoke detector (on the 2nd floor - none on the main floor or in the basement) however did not see any carbon monoxide (CO) detectors. Please ensure both types of detectors are always installed &amp; operational in your home, and not beyond their "replace by" date.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Ventilation



Your home currently has the following exhaust ventilation:

        - The kitchen range hood (does not vent to the outside)

        - There is no bathroom exhaust fan

        - The clothes dryer does vent to the outside



If you decide to install a range hood fan (that vents to the outside) or a bathroom exhaust fan, choose ENERGY STAR?????? qualified units. For a new bathroom fan, consider installing a timer switch (where you select 5, 10, 15, 20 minutes) so it will only remain on for an adequate time to remove bathroom moisture and/or odours without the risk of being left on all day and wasting heat.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Fuel Costs ????????? Fort Providence



The following Dec. 1, 2021 values, including GST, have been used to calculate energy costs and savings in Fort Providence:



Electricity - $18.00 min, $0.31 per kWh up to monthly subsidy threshold, $0.69 per kWh thereafter 

Oil - $1.23 per liter 

Propane - $0.85 per liter delivered

Wood - $100 per cord self-harvested

Pellets - $4.95 per bag, $278.11 per tonne bulk delivered



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Disclaimer



The foregoing report is provided as an assessment of the visible components only and shall not be taken as a guarantee or an acceptance of liability for future performance of any part of the building. This report is prepared with reasonable skill and care within the limitations of a visual inspection on the inspection date. This evaluation and report do not cover code compliance issues set by the authority having jurisdiction.



